---
import { getImage, Image } from 'astro:assets';
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import PhotoHero from '../components/PhotoHero.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Split from '../components/Split.astro';
import QuoteSection from '../components/QuoteSection.astro';
import { img } from '../lib/images';

const heroImage = img('home/story.jpg');
const ogImage = await getImage({ src: heroImage, width: 1200, format: 'webp' });

const houseImage = img('retreats/owners_house.jpg');
const terracesImage = img('farm/pergola.jpg');
const harmonyImage = img('story/roof_construction.jpg');
---

<PageLayout
  title="Our Story — Wild on the Farm | Boquete Cloud Forest Lodge"
  description="How a hotelier from Quito traded the hospitality business for a mountainside in Boquete, and spent eighteen years building an organic farm before ever opening its doors to guests."
  path="/story/"
  image={ogImage.src}
  pullIntro="Wild on the Farm was born from a deep journey through Panama's wildest places. After years of exploration, the founder discovered this untouched land — and saw in it the perfect setting for a self-sustaining haven, where peace, nature and the art of living harmonise."
>
  <Fragment slot="hero">
    <PhotoHero image={heroImage} alt="A living roof grown into the hillside at Wild on the Farm" eyebrow="Our History">
      Born from a journey through the wild.
    </PhotoHero>
  </Fragment>

  <Section label="A Journey to Harmony" heading="In the arms of this great nature">
    <Split>
      <div class="split-photo">
        <Image
          src={harmonyImage}
          alt="Building the living roof at Wild on the Farm, high above the terraced hillside"
          widths={[400, 700, 1000]}
          sizes="(min-width: 860px) 50vw, 100vw"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </div>
      <div>
        <p>Born from exploration, built with love and vision.</p>
        <p>
          Sixteen years ago, I travelled the jungles, mountains and national parks of Panama for
          three years — camping in my hammock and voluntarily losing myself in these spaces
          which, from a life lived around the world, represent my cozy nest: the place where I
          feel finally in complete harmony.
        </p>
        <p>
          So when, by the greatest of chances, coming out of the cloud forest, I discovered this
          place abandoned for eight years, I immediately saw what I had always been looking for —
          to settle, and realise the most beautiful work a person can create: an autonomous place
          in perfect synergy with wild nature.
        </p>
        <p>
          What first fascinated me was the microclimate. Like wrapping myself in the arms of this
          great nature, the place was completely monastic, and corresponded to a much-sought-after
          peace — while opening onto a grandiose view of the Barú volcano and the extraordinary
          dance of the clouds. The soil was virgin, ready to welcome a work of life.
        </p>
        <p>
          I had before me the time and the means — and I could let my mind experiment again with
          the art of living.
        </p>
        <p>— The Founder, Wild on the Farm</p>
      </div>
    </Split>
  </Section>

  <Section label="Finding This Mountain" heading="A slope nobody wanted, at the edge of a national park.">
    <Split>
      <div class="split-photo">
        <Image
          src={houseImage}
          alt="The original house on the property that would become Wild on the Farm"
          widths={[400, 700, 1000]}
          sizes="(min-width: 860px) 50vw, 100vw"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </div>
      <div>
        <p>
          Living in the tropics — I first experienced it in Africa, where I spent three years in
          the seventies with hunter-gatherers, in traditional dwellings, under a fierce heat. I
          lived it in the Amazon in '81 with the Shuar, when the authorities arrested me for
          espionage on the Ecuador-Peru border, where I was paddling down the Río Napo in my
          dugout canoe — I fled, escaping with the indigenous people. I lived the tropics for
          twelve years in Roatán, arriving on the island long before the rush, living like
          Robinson Crusoe on West Bay Beach, swimming with dolphins in impossibly clear water. I
          lived them on every deserted beach in Mexico, before the invaders came craving
          cocktails and noise.
        </p>
        <p>
          I've known them nearly everywhere in the world, and the tropics — you truly live them
          with experience, at the cool remove of altitude, on a road straight out of Disney
          World, one you set out on knowing full well it leads to paradise….
        </p>
        <p>Welcome to my ultimate paradise.</p>
      </div>
    </Split>
  </Section>

  <Section label="Built Slowly, On Purpose" heading="Eighteen years before the first guest ever arrived.">
    <Split reverse>
      <div class="split-photo">
        <Image
          src={terracesImage}
          alt="A pergola on the terraced farm property at Wild on the Farm"
          widths={[400, 700, 1000]}
          sizes="(min-width: 860px) 50vw, 100vw"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </div>
      <div>
        <p>
          What followed wasn't a renovation — it was a farm, built one terrace at a time. Solar
          panels went up before there was anyone to power them for. The macadamia trees were
          planted knowing they wouldn't bear fruit for fifteen years. Sheep and hens moved in to
          work the ground long before there was a dining room to feed from it.
        </p>
        <p>
          It wasn't until 2025 — eighteen years in — that three handcrafted cabins opened their
          doors, built into the hillside the same patient way everything else here was: with time,
          rather than around a deadline.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Split>
  </Section>

  <QuoteSection cite="— Pascal, Founder">
    "I didn't want to build a place that looked like it belonged on this mountain. I wanted to
    build one that actually did — and that meant letting the farm come first, guests second."
  </QuoteSection>

  <Fragment slot="footer">
    <div class="section-label">Continue Exploring</div>
    <h2>See what that patience built.</h2>
    <p>Eighteen years of farming, three cabins, and a kitchen that starts a few steps from the door.</p>
    <div class="contact-list">
      <a href="/farm/">The Farm →</a>
      <a href="/lodge/">The Lodge →</a>
      <a href="/food/">The Food →</a>
    </div>
  </Fragment>
</PageLayout>
